<h2>A Village Within the City</h2>
<p><strong>Bukchon Hanok Village</strong> (북촌한옥마을) sits on a hillside between Gyeongbokgung Palace and Changdeokgung Palace, right in the heart of Seoul. The name means "North Village" — it's north of Cheonggyecheon Stream, which historically divided the city. For six centuries, this was where yangban (aristocracy) and government officials lived in large wooden estates called hanok.</p>

<p>Today, around 900 hanok remain, most of them on the steep alleys of Gahoe-dong. Unlike other traditional villages in Korea that are entirely preserved as tourist sites, Bukchon is a working residential neighborhood. People live here, raise families here, and go to work each morning past tourists who've come to photograph their homes. This creates both the authenticity and the tension that defines the area.</p>

<h2>Getting There</h2>
<p>Take Line 3 (orange) to <strong>Anguk Station</strong>, Exit 3. From the exit, you're already at the edge of the village. The main attraction — the famous view down Bukchon-ro 11-gil — is about a 10-minute walk uphill from the station. Google Maps works well here; follow "Bukchon Hanok Village" and it'll take you to the viewpoint.</p>

<h2>The Famous Viewpoint</h2>
<p>The photo you've seen a thousand times — tiled rooftops cascading down a narrow alley toward Namsan Tower in the distance — is taken from <strong>Bukchon-ro 11-gil</strong>, specifically the section between the two bends in the alley. There's usually a small crowd there. It's worth the walk up regardless of the crowds; the view is genuinely spectacular.</p>

<p>For less-photographed but equally beautiful views, try the alleys around <strong>Gahoe-dong 31</strong> — a slightly different angle with fewer people and the same dramatic roofscape. The northern alleys (toward Samcheong-dong) are quieter still and offer views back toward Bugaksan mountain.</p>

<div class="tip-box">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Arrive before 9am for the best light and fewest crowds. By 11am on weekends, the famous alley is packed. Early morning in autumn or winter, when mist sits in the valley below, Bukchon looks absolutely magical. Weekday mornings are significantly calmer than weekends year-round.</div>

<h2>Hanbok Rental</h2>
<p>Wearing a hanbok (한복, traditional Korean dress) through the village is one of the most enjoyable tourist activities in Seoul. Rental shops are clustered around Anguk Station and on the streets leading into the village. Expect to pay ₩15,000–30,000 for a 3-4 hour rental, which usually includes a simple hair styling service.</p>

<p>The hanbok experience has become so popular that it's reshaped how the village looks — on weekends, the alleys are filled with people in colorful silk. It's slightly surreal but also genuinely fun, and the photos come out beautifully against the traditional architecture.</p>

<h3>Best Hanbok Rental Spots</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hanboknam</strong> — good quality hanbok, friendly staff, located near Anguk Station</li>
  <li><strong>Gyeongbokgung Hanbok</strong> — if you're visiting the palace, several shops on the north side offer palace-entry packages (wearing hanbok gives free palace admission)</li>
  <li><strong>Leebok</strong> — slightly pricier but excellent quality, with a range of styles from classical to modern-traditional fusion</li>
</ul>

<h2>Walking the Alleys</h2>
<p>The village is best explored on foot, and it rewards slow wandering over systematic sightseeing. The main alleys are numbered (Bukchon 1-ro through 8-ro), but the best discoveries are in the unnamed lanes between them. Look for:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Traditional gates (솟을대문) with their distinctive curved rooflines</li>
  <li>Persimmon trees in courtyards, especially beautiful in autumn</li>
  <li>Small community bulletin boards showing village events and neighborhood notices</li>
  <li>The occasional workshop with the door slightly open — some residents are traditional craftspeople</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Bukchon is a quiet residential area. The village has put up signs in multiple languages asking visitors to speak quietly, not to sit on steps or lean on walls, and not to peer into windows or courtyards. Follow these — the residents have been remarkably patient about sharing their neighborhood, but the situation has caused genuine friction in recent years.</div>

<h2>Samcheong-dong: The Perfect Pairing</h2>
<p>Just north of Bukchon, <strong>Samcheong-dong</strong> is a neighborhood of galleries, upscale cafés, and charming restaurants set along a tree-lined street. After exploring the hanok alleys, walking into Samcheong-dong for lunch or coffee is a natural next step.</p>

<p>The <strong>National Folk Museum of Korea</strong> is at the edge of Gyeongbokgung Palace grounds and offers free admission — a good introduction to traditional Korean daily life if you want some cultural context for what you're seeing in the village.</p>

<h2>Changdeokgung Palace & Huwon</h2>
<p>At the eastern edge of Bukchon is <strong>Changdeokgung Palace</strong>, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and arguably the most beautiful of Seoul's five palaces. The secret garden (<strong>Huwon</strong>, 후원) behind the palace is a 78-acre woodland garden that was reserved for royalty for centuries. Admission to the palace is ₩3,000; the secret garden tour (in English) costs ₩8,000 and must be booked in advance. It's one of the most serene places in the city.</p>

